": "D4 dropped Black's lead from +0.1 to -10.5, a 10.6-point loss. The engine wanted F2 on the bottom edge instead. This was the first of three costly moves in a row."}, {"turn": 20, "text": "White's D5 gave the points straight back, moving the lead from -10.5 to +0.4 and losing 10.8 points. The engine's choice was F2, the point neither side had taken yet. The 20k let Black off the hook."}, {"turn": 23, "text": "G2 was the worst move of the game: -7.6 to -26.8, a 19.2-point loss. White's stone on F2 was already in place, and the engine's choice was E2. Black's lead was only -0.3 at move 15 and had been sinking since."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "White's E2 lost 20.7 points, taking the lead from -26.8 to -6.1. The engine preferred D2. The 20k's mistake cancelled nearly all of Black's, but Black never got back to even."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran either called the game tools to play a move it had chosen by hand, or worked on the current board and on records: game-093-inspection.js counted groups, liberties and area, game-093-verification.js replayed the actual moves to check legality and captures, and the rest wrote the log, SGF, review and memory notes. Nothing looped over candidate moves, played continuations forward or ranked moves, and the 'hypothetical lines' were only described in the written notes.", "stop": false}}